ore either Guapo but the rules are  broken so deftly that it's never jarring  editor Jim mo yang has spoken  specifically about his role in removing  unnecessary scenes and it really shows  transitions like this one she's doing  you swoon did you go to a table a baby  didn't mean I told you guys Shirley or  this one but not whose you can say  you're not on day  are responsible for much of the film's  efficient timing and pace that help it  feel all the more intense and thrilling  enough do you think I'm ugly yes  Jojo rabbit the only comedy film  nominated uses editing deathly and  cleverly in many places I love watching  a film where the editor feels free to  have some fun why people do this and it  doesn't break or hurt my immersion in  the story  Georgia rabbit has to manage some  difficult tonal shifts from comedy to  it's darker dramatic moments and editor  Tom Eagles manages these transitions  well  [Music]  I want to show you one of my favorite  little moments from the film it's a  great example of how editing can help  turn a brief line in the script into  something more interesting and  meaningful  [Music]  it's moments like this that illustrate  this sort of ineffable quality that  editing can have sometimes or just the  timing of cuts communicates a feeling  that would be difficult to put into  words  so which film should win while judging  between good and bad editing in general  is pretty doable judging best editing is  a fool's errand for many reasons  primarily there's no way of knowing how  well the editors did compared to one  another unless we read each script and  saw all the footage used to craft the  film if you're taking into consideration  how much work went on behind the scenes  you could talk about the difficulty of  assembling dramatic racing sequences out  of hours of footage for Ford versus  Ferrari you could talk about the added  challenge of crafting scenes around the  performances of child actors in Jojo  rabbit you could talk about the  challenges of choosing the best approach  for each scene from the variety of  improvisational performances Joaquin  Phoenix brought too many scenes and  Joker  you could try to say gin mo yang did  less work on parasite because of how  closely the film adheres to bong Joon  hos storyboards and the lack of coverage  used in shooting the film I personally  think the best and fairest way is to  consider just what we see on the screen  the timing and progression of one shot  to the next and the role that plays in  telling the story for me the two major  contenders are the Irishman and parasite  Ford versus Ferrari has the macro pacing  issues I mentioned Jojo rabbit and Joker  are well edited but for the winner of  this award I want the editing to have  played a clear role in what made the  film great the editing in the irishman  parasite are absolutely essential to how  successful those films are at telling  their stories without a deft experienced  hand the Irishman's massive scope  parenthetical story and larger and samel  scenes could have easily fallen apart  under the weight of their running time  or become confusing but instead it's  layers and complexities are easy to  ingest the pace only becomes heavy when  it's de matically relevant to the story  it's difficult to pick between the two  and I'd be happy to see the award go to  the Irishman but ultimately I think I'll  pick parasite the flow of the film's  pace as they increases tension and  escalates is flawless  even while it juggles multiple  characters over many locations it has  zero deadweight or unnecessary running  time no moment on screen goes unused  ultimately well places it at the top for  me as that it has a quality that the  Academy often likes to overlook its  editing feels effortless and is  difficult to even notice  with only 960 cuts in the film well  below the average of 1,250 the editing  of parasite is economical but incredibly  effective if it won it'd be a great  example of choosing a 